+findings:
+  - id: H1
+    category: harness
+    severity: P3
+    title: "Inside a drive the box is raised and committed while its decision is only proposed; the play does not say what dropping the decision does to the raised box, and a later capability in the same drive reads the raised ceiling"
+    path: core/components/plays/understand/reference/ice.md
+    also: core/components/plays/understand/SKILL.md
+    principle: "A box-move has two halves, the raised profile level and its decision. The write-then-review rule guarantees nothing out-of-box is committed without approval and declares exceptions only for /intent and /adr; the roll-up reads the committed profile level as the ceiling, so a committed raise stands for the next run. A play that commits the raise on a deferral must say how the drive's review can undo it."
+    source: "main:core/components/memory/standards/rules/direct-model-write.md ('Order of operations' final paragraph, 'Declared exception' paragraphs for /intent and /adr); main:core/components/plays/understand/scripts/rollup_box.py (header: committed level); main:core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/decision.yaml (status values proposed | accepted | superseded | rejected); main:docs/adr/031-business-intent-and-ice.md part 5 (the same 'confirm or drop at the end review' shape, with alignment cleaning up after a drop)"
+    detail: >
+      C8 and C13 say each box-move is written with its decision proposed and 'the drive's review accepts or
+      drops it'. But the keyed persist has already raised the profile dimension, and Step 7 commits that raise
